WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.533 --> 00:00:02.469
This news just in to CNN

00:00:02.469 --> 00:00:03.069
in our health lead.

00:00:03.069 --> 00:00:05.138
The Supreme Court has blocked Purdue

00:00:05.138 --> 00:00:06.840
Pharma, the massive manufacturer

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.341
of OxyContin,

00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:09.376
from going forward

00:00:09.376 --> 00:00:10.343
with bankruptcy

00:00:10.343 --> 00:00:11.011
proceedings

00:00:11.011 --> 00:00:13.380
and a $6.6 billion settlement.

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.181
This was part of an arrangement

00:00:15.181 --> 00:00:16.850
that would ultimately offer

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:18.318
the Sackler family,

00:00:18.318 --> 00:00:19.386
the founders of Purdue

00:00:19.386 --> 00:00:21.354
Pharma, Broad protection

00:00:21.354 --> 00:00:23.823
from opioid related civil claims.

00:00:23.823 --> 00:00:25.458
The Supreme Court says

00:00:25.458 --> 00:00:26.126
they will now

00:00:26.126 --> 00:00:28.261
take up this case and hear arguments

00:00:28.261 --> 00:00:30.130
about it in December.

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:32.198
Let's bring in CNN's Jean Casarez

00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:33.266
and Joan Biskupic.

00:00:33.266 --> 00:00:33.700
Jean,

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:36.036
what exactly was in this settlement?

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:40.006
Well, first of all, when it began in 2019

00:00:40.006 --> 00:00:41.174
when the bankruptcy

00:00:41.174 --> 00:00:43.810
was declared by Purdue Pharma,

00:00:44.711 --> 00:00:45.912
all of the civil suits

00:00:45.912 --> 00:00:47.947
and there were so many around the country

00:00:47.947 --> 00:00:49.182
against the Sacklers,

00:00:49.182 --> 00:00:50.283
against Purdue, Pharma,

00:00:50.283 --> 00:00:51.751
they all were channeled

00:00:51.751 --> 00:00:53.653
into the bankruptcy action.

00:00:53.653 --> 00:00:54.854
That was one of the first things

00:00:54.854 --> 00:00:55.789
they did.

00:00:55.789 --> 00:00:56.823
And people need to realize

00:00:56.823 --> 00:00:58.625
how huge this was.

00:00:58.625 --> 00:01:00.160
This involves states

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:01.461
This involved counties.

00:01:01.461 --> 00:01:02.929
This involved townships.

00:01:02.929 --> 00:01:04.697
This involves tribal nations

00:01:04.697 --> 00:01:06.099
all over the country.

00:01:06.099 --> 00:01:07.467
I remember being in the courtroom

00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:08.301
and it was

00:01:08.301 --> 00:01:10.036
filled with attorneys, attorneys

00:01:10.036 --> 00:01:12.272
by phone all over the country.

00:01:12.272 --> 00:01:14.074
So they started negotiating.

00:01:14.074 --> 00:01:14.374
They've been

00:01:14.374 --> 00:01:18.211
negotiating for years on this since 2019.

00:01:18.211 --> 00:01:20.080
And they finally determine

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.282
And and there was a handful of states

00:01:22.282 --> 00:01:24.084
that were opposed to this.

00:01:24.084 --> 00:01:25.652
But it was really symbolically

00:01:25.652 --> 00:01:26.519
at the end,

00:01:26.519 --> 00:01:28.788
they decided that what would happen

00:01:28.788 --> 00:01:31.024
is that the Sacklers would pay out

00:01:31.024 --> 00:01:33.326
approximately $6 billion

00:01:33.326 --> 00:01:35.161
to individual claimants,

00:01:35.161 --> 00:01:38.832
to states, to abatement procedures

00:01:38.832 --> 00:01:41.234
involving crisis medications

00:01:41.234 --> 00:01:43.103
that could be given to people.

00:01:43.103 --> 00:01:44.737
And in lieu of that

00:01:44.737 --> 00:01:45.405
and their names

00:01:45.405 --> 00:01:46.739
would be taken off buildings

00:01:46.739 --> 00:01:47.574
around the country.

00:01:47.574 --> 00:01:49.175
Also that was part of it.

00:01:49.175 --> 00:01:50.477
But in lieu of that,

00:01:50.477 --> 00:01:52.645
they would not face civil suits

00:01:52.645 --> 00:01:53.713
going forward

00:01:53.713 --> 00:01:56.883
because the money would be going toward

00:01:57.617 --> 00:01:59.486
everything that I just described.

00:01:59.486 --> 00:02:01.821
And here's the impact of this.

00:02:02.422 --> 00:02:05.191
Those people that were the victims

00:02:05.191 --> 00:02:05.859
and that

00:02:05.859 --> 00:02:06.426
they wanted

00:02:06.426 --> 00:02:06.993
money

00:02:06.993 --> 00:02:09.629
and $6 billion is going to all of that.

00:02:09.662 --> 00:02:11.498
This will be stopped in its tracks.

00:02:11.498 --> 00:02:12.332
So no one,

00:02:12.332 --> 00:02:13.533
no community, no

00:02:13.533 --> 00:02:14.667
abatement proceedings,

00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:16.870
no medications will be going

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:17.470
for the short

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:20.640
term to those entities that so need it.

00:02:21.241 --> 00:02:23.743
Joan, are you surprised that the U.S.

00:02:23.743 --> 00:02:24.210
Supreme Court

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:26.346
has not only blocked this settlement

00:02:26.346 --> 00:02:28.281
that had been agreed upon,

00:02:28.281 --> 00:02:29.549
but that they're also going to

00:02:29.549 --> 00:02:31.017
take up the case?

00:02:31.017 --> 00:02:32.152
Well, Jake,

00:02:32.152 --> 00:02:34.287
those are pretty dramatic terms

00:02:34.287 --> 00:02:35.989
that Jeanne just outlined.

00:02:35.989 --> 00:02:37.557
And the solicitor

00:02:37.557 --> 00:02:38.591
general of the United States

00:02:38.591 --> 00:02:41.027
actually used them in her arguments

00:02:41.027 --> 00:02:42.562
to try to get the Supreme Court

00:02:42.562 --> 00:02:43.897
to intervene, to say

00:02:43.897 --> 00:02:46.232
this is a very exceptional agreement,

00:02:46.533 --> 00:02:48.635
unprecedented.

00:02:48.635 --> 00:02:50.570
The scope of it is something that

00:02:52.238 --> 00:02:53.673
maybe should be blocked.

00:02:53.673 --> 00:02:55.241
And in encouraging

00:02:55.241 --> 00:02:56.276
the justices to take it

00:02:56.276 --> 00:02:59.045
up, say look at lower courts to divide

00:03:00.113 --> 00:03:01.714
out there on situations

00:03:01.714 --> 00:03:03.216
when companies, when parties

00:03:03.216 --> 00:03:05.218
can be released from liability.

00:03:05.218 --> 00:03:06.886
So it is in some ways, it's

00:03:06.886 --> 00:03:08.288
surprising that they're intervening

00:03:08.288 --> 00:03:09.222
at this stage.

00:03:09.222 --> 00:03:10.990
But this is the most crucial stage.

00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:12.058
And they have set it

00:03:12.058 --> 00:03:14.027
on a pretty fast track.

00:03:14.027 --> 00:03:16.162
They've ordered a fairly quick

00:03:16.162 --> 00:03:17.096
briefing schedule.

00:03:17.096 --> 00:03:17.597
And the case

00:03:17.597 --> 00:03:18.598
would be heard

00:03:18.598 --> 00:03:20.166
in early December

00:03:20.166 --> 00:03:21.668
with a resolution, Jake,

00:03:21.668 --> 00:03:24.637
probably by the end of June of next year.

00:03:24.637 --> 00:03:25.972
So, you know,

00:03:25.972 --> 00:03:27.674
when you think of the opioid

00:03:27.674 --> 00:03:29.142
opioid addiction

00:03:29.142 --> 00:03:30.710
crisis in America

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:33.213
and the responsibility of Purdue Pharma

00:03:33.213 --> 00:03:35.381
and the Sackler family,

00:03:35.381 --> 00:03:36.082
this is a

00:03:36.082 --> 00:03:37.951
pretty unprecedented settlement.

00:03:37.951 --> 00:03:39.152
So that's why the Supreme Court

00:03:39.152 --> 00:03:41.287
has intervened at this point Jake.

00:03:41.287 --> 00:03:41.754
All right, Joan

00:03:41.754 --> 00:03:43.189
Biskupic and Jean Casarez, thanks.

00:03:43.189 --> 00:03:43.323
Let's

00:03:43.323 --> 00:03:46.226
bring in the attorney general of Ohio.

00:03:46.559 --> 00:03:47.160
Dave Yost.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.295
Ohio is one of the eight states

00:03:49.295 --> 00:03:49.629
as well

00:03:49.629 --> 00:03:50.597
as Washington, D.C.,

00:03:50.597 --> 00:03:51.364
that are part

00:03:51.364 --> 00:03:53.466
of the $6 billion settlement.

00:03:53.466 --> 00:03:54.701
And you might remember Ohio

00:03:54.701 --> 00:03:55.635
as, of course,

00:03:55.635 --> 00:03:57.070
tragically, one of the epicenters

00:03:57.070 --> 00:03:58.638
of the opioid crisis.

00:03:58.638 --> 00:04:00.139
General Yost, thanks for joining us.

00:04:00.139 --> 00:04:01.708
Did you want the Supreme Court

00:04:01.708 --> 00:04:03.042
to take up the case

00:04:03.042 --> 00:04:05.378
or did you want them to allow the

00:04:05.378 --> 00:04:06.479
settlement to move forward?

00:04:08.047 --> 00:04:10.850
We one of the settlement to move forward.

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:11.551
This is money

00:04:11.551 --> 00:04:13.586
that it's not flowing to individual

00:04:13.586 --> 00:04:15.922
claimant families. It's not coming back.

00:04:16.256 --> 00:04:18.558
Equally important to our communities

00:04:18.591 --> 00:04:19.759
and to our states

00:04:19.759 --> 00:04:21.160
to fight back

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:23.696
against this avalanche of addiction

00:04:23.696 --> 00:04:27.033
that was architected by the Sacklers.

00:04:27.834 --> 00:04:30.136
What is the

00:04:30.136 --> 00:04:32.872
I'm going to ask you a kind of a question

00:04:32.872 --> 00:04:34.507
I haven't asked anybody before.

00:04:34.507 --> 00:04:36.376
What's the best argument

00:04:36.376 --> 00:04:37.977
for the people who disagree with you?

00:04:37.977 --> 00:04:39.579
The best argument being made by,

00:04:39.579 --> 00:04:41.214
for instance, the solicitor general.

00:04:41.214 --> 00:04:42.582
People who want the Supreme Court

00:04:42.582 --> 00:04:43.783
to review this case.

00:04:43.783 --> 00:04:44.350
They they

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:46.219
they disagree with the settlement.

00:04:46.219 --> 00:04:48.254
I realize I'm asking you to state

00:04:48.254 --> 00:04:49.122
something you don't agree

00:04:49.122 --> 00:04:52.558
with, but what is their best case?

00:04:53.092 --> 00:04:53.726
Well,

00:04:53.726 --> 00:04:54.894
I think there's probably

00:04:54.894 --> 00:04:56.829
two arguments on the other side.

00:04:56.829 --> 00:04:58.898
The first is that there's a lack of

00:04:58.898 --> 00:05:01.034
due process in the in the

00:05:02.101 --> 00:05:03.870
discharging that it's just

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:05.938
fundamentally unfair

00:05:05.938 --> 00:05:08.274
to let the Sacklers walk away.

00:05:09.142 --> 00:05:10.310
The problem with that

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:12.612
is that under the law,

00:05:13.413 --> 00:05:15.648
we can get to Purdue Pharma.

00:05:15.648 --> 00:05:19.552
The individual family money that's behind

00:05:19.552 --> 00:05:21.120
that is much more difficult

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:23.323
legally in most of our states.

00:05:23.323 --> 00:05:24.090
The other issue,

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:26.492
which is kind of a policy issue, is

00:05:26.859 --> 00:05:28.561
what do we do with these mass torts

00:05:28.561 --> 00:05:31.164
that are so massive?

00:05:31.164 --> 00:05:33.866
There are existential lawsuits

00:05:33.866 --> 00:05:36.069
And to maximize

00:05:36.069 --> 00:05:39.172
the recovery for the wrongs done.

00:05:39.172 --> 00:05:42.342
You have to have some kind of means

00:05:42.342 --> 00:05:44.610
of marshaling all these claims.

00:05:46.112 --> 00:05:48.348
And this is going to be

00:05:48.348 --> 00:05:49.315
an interesting argument

00:05:49.315 --> 00:05:50.850
of the Supreme Court

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:54.020
and your argument is correct me

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:55.321
if I'm wrong.

00:05:55.321 --> 00:05:57.056
There are people in Ohio

00:05:57.056 --> 00:05:59.792
and the other states who need help now

00:05:59.926 --> 00:06:01.394
who need that money now.

00:06:01.394 --> 00:06:03.496
Organizations that need to help people

00:06:03.496 --> 00:06:04.964
who are still suffering

00:06:04.964 --> 00:06:05.832
in this epidemic

00:06:05.832 --> 00:06:06.699
that continues

00:06:06.699 --> 00:06:08.401
to take thousands of lives a year.

00:06:08.401 --> 00:06:08.835
Is that right?

00:06:10.737 --> 00:06:12.405
That's absolutely correct.

00:06:12.405 --> 00:06:15.308
And as the court of Appeals said,

00:06:15.708 --> 00:06:19.245
bankruptcy is a creature of compromises.

00:06:20.012 --> 00:06:20.780
It's it's

00:06:20.780 --> 00:06:21.147
it's a

00:06:21.147 --> 00:06:24.250
messy process of competing interests.

00:06:25.151 --> 00:06:26.552
We've seen some preliminary data

00:06:26.552 --> 00:06:27.887
showing that drug overdose deaths

00:06:27.887 --> 00:06:28.855
have leveled off,

00:06:28.855 --> 00:06:31.924
but still more than 100,000 Americans

00:06:31.924 --> 00:06:33.393
losing their lives from overdoses

00:06:33.393 --> 00:06:34.360
nationally, most of them,

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:37.296
I believe, opioids.

00:06:37.296 --> 00:06:39.632
How is Ohio working

00:06:39.966 --> 00:06:43.469
to alleviate the crisis?

00:06:44.137 --> 00:06:44.871
Well,

00:06:45.338 --> 00:06:48.107
we have a coalition,

00:06:48.975 --> 00:06:51.144
we call it the one Ohio Foundation

00:06:51.144 --> 00:06:53.312
that has all of our local government

00:06:53.413 --> 00:06:54.447
and the state government.

00:06:54.447 --> 00:06:56.082
We're sharing the money

00:06:56.082 --> 00:06:57.650
from these various lawsuits

00:06:57.650 --> 00:06:59.685
to bring it back to the local level

00:06:59.886 --> 00:07:02.789
for treatment for poor, for prevention,

00:07:03.790 --> 00:07:07.894
for enforcement, because this war is

00:07:07.894 --> 00:07:09.228
fought on the ground

00:07:09.228 --> 00:07:10.963
of our streets, of our cities

00:07:10.963 --> 00:07:12.632
and our counties

00:07:12.632 --> 00:07:15.067
and it's so important

00:07:15.067 --> 00:07:16.502
to get this money flowing.

00:07:16.502 --> 00:07:18.604
It's been since 2019

00:07:19.272 --> 00:07:20.873
that this bankruptcy proceedings

00:07:20.873 --> 00:07:21.641
been going on.

00:07:21.641 --> 00:07:23.109
I guess the best thing I can say

00:07:23.109 --> 00:07:25.711
about today's decision to hear the case

00:07:25.978 --> 00:07:28.147
is at least they set it for December.

00:07:28.881 --> 00:07:29.515
Yeah.

00:07:29.515 --> 00:07:30.149
Can you imagine

00:07:30.149 --> 00:07:31.083
if terrorists killed

00:07:31.083 --> 00:07:32.585
100,000 Americans a year

00:07:32.585 --> 00:07:34.787
But this is us doing it to ourselves.

00:07:34.787 --> 00:07:36.222
Ohio Attorney General Dave Yost,

00:07:36.222 --> 00:07:37.757
thank you so much for your time.

00:07:37.757 --> 00:07:38.958
As always, we appreciate it.

00:07:40.393 --> 00:07:40.927
Thank you, sir.

